  "notes": "The research calls this \"the crossover exception in this lane\" and names the reason: major and mixolydian as often as minor. That inverts the scale table against every sibling here — `major` outweighs `natural_minor` — and the progression families carry two bright ones against a single hard i–VII–VI. The hook is described as a crowd chant *doubling the loop's top line*, which is precisely `octave_echo`, so the countermelody is weighted onto it at a high density ratio rather than onto the answer lick the rest of the axis uses; the chant-before-verse-1 structure is why `arrangement` is overridden at all, with the hook-first order weighted above the verse-first one. Bass has real motion — root, 5th, octave — so `independent_riff` with `P5` and `P8` as the passing tones and no others, which is a literal reading rather than a jazz walk. `percs.lanes` carries the tambourine the research puts on the 8ths in hooks. ⚠ `medium` because the research rates it medium-high and its producer interview is weighted toward Kay Gee's R&B work rather than these records. The inferred parameters are the swing amount and the open-hat rate; the research gives neither.",
